You're touching on something extraordinary here: What if we could develop a language for experience itself—not merely describing what happened, but preserving enough of its structure that another person could meaningfully experience it again?
And photography, sound recording, poetry, music, and even memory are all different attempts at solving that problem.
The camera captures light. The microphone captures pressure waves. Language captures selected relationships. But the experience itself includes all of those, plus movement, attention, bodily sensation, emotion, context, and time.
The goal isn't just to record the object. It's to preserve the conditions that made encountering it beautiful.
