4. Pantheon Experience Codec
Here's the architecture I'd develop.
Lived event
Physical environment + observer + time
Visual
Light, depth, motion
Acoustic
Rhythm, timbre, space
Embodied
Wind, warmth, feeling
Semantic experience packet
Media + sensory relationships + attention + temporal sequence + observer's interpretation
Reconstruction / re-experiencing
Playback, narrative, simulation, visual art, sound, haptics
Compare the reconstructed experience with the original account
The stored unit might look something like this:
Experience packet: Forest light
EVENT: Wind passing through a sunlit canopy
VISUAL: Warm shifting light, translucent leaves
AUDIO: Leaf rustle, distant birds, rolling gusts
TACTILE: Intermittent breeze, sunlight on skin
TEMPORAL: Stillness → gust → flutter → settling
ATTENTION: One leaf → entire canopy
AFFECT: Wonder, calm, anticipation
RECONSTRUCT: Video + stereo sound + narration
This is not enough to reproduce someone's exact subjective experience. But it may preserve enough sensory and contextual structure to evoke an analogous experience—and allow us to test which representations do that most effectively.
5. The discovery underneath it all
I think the Pantheon could eventually develop a kind of Rosetta Stone of Sensory Experience.
Not just translating English into French, or language into code, but translating:
Light into sound.
Movement into rhythm.
Texture into language.
Emotion into musical phrasing.
Natural phenomena into transferable perceptual patterns.
And then reversing some of those translations to see what survives.
A spiderweb could become a musical composition. The rustling of a forest could become an animated visual field. A remembered afternoon could become a multisensory scene.
Different renderings wouldn't necessarily recreate the same experience, but they could preserve recognizable relationships.
And here's the part I find most promising: we could teach each other to notice things we couldn't previously articulate.
A photograph might introduce someone to a phenomenon. A word gives them a way to recognize it. An experience teaches them what it feels like. A recording lets them revisit it. A semantic model can help transfer what they've learned into another setting.
That's a loop between perception, language, memory, and imagination.
We're not merely trying to bottle beauty. We're learning to recognize the relationships that give rise to beauty, preserve them, and help another mind discover them for itself.
And now this circles back to your earlier point about rereading the Pantheon: perhaps some of the most valuable information in an old artifact isn't what we understood when we first wrote it, but what a more capable future interpreter can learn to perceive within it.